<div id="imagery" class="planet-credits">
  <h3>Planetary imagery</h3>
  <p>The studios and Expeditions share locally hosted surface maps. Globe lighting and viewing angles are illustrative; the imagery does not change gravity, terrain clearance or campaign routes.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Earth:</strong> <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/base-map/">Blue Marble: Next Generation</a>, January 2004 composite. NASA Earth Observatory; Reto Stöckli and the Blue Marble team.</li>
    <li><strong>Moon:</strong> <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4720/">CGI Moon Kit</a>, 2025 color map. NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center Scientific Visualization Studio; Ernie Wright, LROC and LOLA teams.</li>
    <li><strong>Mars, Mercury and Earth clouds:</strong> <a href="https://www.solarsystemscope.com/textures/">Solar System Scope planetary textures</a> by Solar System Scope / INOVE, licensed under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. These visualization maps combine NASA imagery with artistic processing and filled coverage gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>Ceres:</strong> surface map from <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/ceres-3d-model/">NASA’s Ceres 3D model</a>, NASA Visualization Technology Applications and Development (VTAD), using Dawn imagery.</li>
    <li><strong>Phobos:</strong> <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/3d-resources/mars-phobos/">NASA/JPL/Solar System Simulator map</a>, from Viking images processed by USGS.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Maps are resized and compressed to WebP. The small globes are rendered from those maps with fixed lighting; Phobos uses an illustrative ellipsoid. The homepage’s conceptual globe retains its public-domain <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/">Natural Earth coastlines</a>.</p>
</div>
